1️⃣ Timeline: From driver to PHY

Driver submits packet

cpdma_chan_submit()


Fills descriptor with:

DMA address (hw_buffer)

Packet length (hw_len)

Mode flags (OWNER, SOP, EOP)

Sets OWNER=1 → hardware now owns descriptor

CPDMA fetches packet from memory

Reads descriptor from DMA-coherent RAM

Reads memory pointed by hw_buffer

Writes data to MAC TX FIFO via bus transfer (AHB)

MAC serializes frame and sends to PHY

MAC logic handles preamble, CRC, PHY interface

PHY transmits bits on wire

2️⃣ When is “TX complete” triggered?

TX completion does NOT wait for PHY to put bits on the wire

It is typically triggered when CPDMA finishes consuming the descriptor — i.e., DMA has fetched all bytes and delivered them to MAC FIFO

This is called descriptor completion

Reason: Waiting for PHY transmission would be slow and unnecessary for driver bookkeeping

3️⃣ How hardware signals completion

CPDMA marks descriptor done

Clears OWNER bit (back to software)

Optionally sets status bits (like error, packet transmitted)

CPDMA can generate an interrupt

Interrupt line to CPU/driver

Indicates one or more descriptors have completed

Driver interrupt handler runs

Function typically called: TX interrupt handler (cpdma_tx_irq or similar)

Iterates over completed descriptors

4️⃣ What driver does on TX completion

For each completed descriptor:

Check status

Transmission success / errors

Unmap DMA memory

dma_unmap_single(dev, desc->sw_buffer, desc->sw_len, DMA_TO_DEVICE);


Removes DMA mapping

Flushes/invalidate caches if necessary

Free descriptor back to pool

cpdma_desc_free(pool, desc, 1);


Returns descriptor to gen_pool

Updates chan->count

Free packet buffer if driver owns it

Typically dev_kfree_skb()

Notify upper layers / net stack

Packet has been “transmitted” from driver point of view

Triggers netif_tx_complete() or netif_wake_queue()

5️⃣ Flow in code (AM335x CPSW TX path)
Driver:
    cpdma_chan_submit()
        ↓
CPDMA engine:
    Reads descriptor → Fetch packet → MAC FIFO
    Clears OWNER → Generates TX done interrupt
        ↓
Interrupt handler:
    cpdma_chan_irq()
        ↓
    For each completed descriptor:
        dma_unmap_single()
        free descriptor (gen_pool)
        free skb
        update stats / notify stack


Important: Descriptor free happens in interrupt context, after hardware releases ownership

DMA buffer (skb->data) is freed only after unmapping

6️⃣ Key points about PHY vs DMA

PHY sending bits = independent hardware process

Driver TX completion is triggered by DMA engine, not PHY

Waiting for PHY to finish would block CPU unnecessarily

MAC/PHY may assert TX complete interrupt for higher-level network events, but for Linux driver, CPDMA completion is sufficient

7️⃣ Summary of TX completion
Step	Who handles it	What happens
Descriptor consumed	CPDMA	DMA reads packet into MAC FIFO, clears OWNER
Interrupt generated	CPDMA → CPU	Signals driver descriptors are done
Descriptor bookkeeping	Driver	Unmaps DMA, frees descriptor, updates stats
Packet buffer cleanup	Driver	Frees skb if necessary
PHY transmission	PHY/MAC	Independent, driver does not wait

✅ TL;DR:

Packet is “sent” from the driver’s point of view as soon as CPDMA finishes using the descriptor

Interrupt is generated by CPDMA, not the PHY

Driver frees descriptor back to pool and unmaps DMA memory

The actual PHY may still be putting bits on the wire, but driver and kernel don’t wait

